      title: "The Life of Geoffrey Chaucer",
      body: `## A wine merchant's son

**Geoffrey Chaucer**, often called the father of English poetry, was born in **London**, probably in the early **1340s**. His father, **John Chaucer**, was a prosperous **vintner**, a wine merchant, who supplied wine to the royal court; the family name, from the French *chaussier*, meant "shoemaker." Chaucer grew up in the bustling world of the London merchant class, in a house on Thames Street, near the docks where wine ships from Bordeaux unloaded their cargoes. He would spend his life moving between that world and the world of the court.

We know a surprising amount about Chaucer's life, because he held many official positions, and nearly five hundred documents mentioning him survive in the royal and city records. But not one of these documents mentions that he was a poet. They record a courtier, soldier, diplomat, and civil servant.

## Page and soldier

The first record of Chaucer, from **1357**, is an account book of the household of **Elizabeth de Burgh**, Countess of Ulster, wife of King Edward III's son **Lionel**, recording a payment for a short cloak, a pair of red and black breeches, and shoes for a young page named Geoffrey Chaucer. Service as a page in a great household was the usual way for a young man of good family to learn courtly manners and make connections.

In **1359–1360**, Chaucer served in King **Edward III**'s army in France, during the **Hundred Years' War**. He was captured near **Reims**, and in March 1360, the king himself contributed **sixteen pounds** to his ransom—less than he paid for a horse, scholars like to note, but a substantial sum. By the mid-1360s, Chaucer was a member of the royal household. Around **1366**, he married **Philippa de Roet**, a lady-in-waiting to the queen, whose sister, **Katherine Swynford**, became the mistress and later the third wife of **John of Gaunt**, duke of Lancaster, the most powerful nobleman in England. Through this connection, Chaucer enjoyed the patronage of the house of Lancaster.

## Diplomat

Chaucer was sent on several **diplomatic missions** abroad, to France, Flanders, Spain, and, most importantly, **Italy**. In **1372–1373**, he traveled to **Genoa** and **Florence**, and in **1378** to **Milan**. In Italy, he encountered the work of the three great Italian writers of the fourteenth century: **Dante**, **Petrarch**, and **Boccaccio**. Whether he met Petrarch or Boccaccio, who were still alive, is unknown, but their works transformed his poetry. He adapted Boccaccio's poems in two of his greatest works, *Troilus and Criseyde* and *The Knight's Tale*, and translated a sonnet of Petrarch, the first in English, into *Troilus*. He was the first English poet to know and use Italian literature.

## Customs official

In **1374**, Chaucer was appointed **Controller of the Wool Custom** in the Port of London, responsible for keeping the accounts of the taxes on exported wool, England's most valuable export and the king's most important source of revenue. He held the post for twelve years. The same year, the city of London granted him a lease, rent-free for life, of a dwelling above **Aldgate**, one of the gates in the city wall. There, in the 1370s and 1380s, he wrote much of his poetry. In *The House of Fame*, the eagle who carries the dreamer to the heavens teases him for spending his days at his accounts and his nights reading books until he is "dazed," living "as an hermyte."

## A troubling document

In **1380**, a woman named **Cecily Champain** signed a legal document releasing Chaucer from all actions concerning her ***raptus***. The Latin word could mean either rape or abduction, and the document has troubled scholars and readers for generations. In **2022**, the scholars **Sebastian Sobecki** and **Euan Roger** announced the discovery of new documents suggesting that the case concerned not sexual assault but a dispute under the **labor laws**: Cecily may have been a servant who left her former employer to work for Chaucer, and her release protected him from a lawsuit by the employer. The interpretation has been widely discussed; it does not resolve all questions, but it has changed the conversation.

## Later years

In **1386**, Chaucer gave up his customs post and his house at Aldgate, perhaps because of political changes, and moved to **Kent**, where he served as a justice of the peace and a member of **Parliament**. In **1389**, King **Richard II** appointed him **Clerk of the King's Works**, responsible for the maintenance of royal buildings, including the Tower of London and Westminster Palace; in this job, he was robbed by highwaymen at least once. In his last years, he received pensions from both Richard II and Richard's supplanter, **Henry IV**, the son of John of Gaunt. One of his last poems, *The Complaint of Chaucer to His Purse*, a witty plea for money, was addressed to Henry IV.

Chaucer died in **1400**, traditionally on **October 25**. He had taken a lease on a house in the precincts of **Westminster Abbey**, and he was buried in the abbey. In **1556**, a later admirer erected a monument over his grave. When the poet **Edmund Spenser** was buried near him in **1599**, the tradition began of burying poets in that part of the abbey, now known as **Poets' Corner**.

## A man of two worlds

Chaucer's life gave him an unusually wide knowledge of English society. He knew the court and the nobility, the merchants and craftsmen of London, the world of law, government, and commerce, soldiers, clergy, and the ordinary people he met in his work at the port. He traveled widely in Europe and read widely in French, Italian, and Latin. All of this experience would flow into *The Canterbury Tales*, with its gallery of characters from every level of society.

> **Pause and consider.** None of the nearly five hundred documents about Chaucer's life mentions his poetry. What does this suggest about the status of a poet in fourteenth-century England?`,
